>
    <label>Apex - Exposed class without sharing</label>
    <protected>true</protected>
    <values>
        <field>Domain__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">ApexClass</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>IsEnabled__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:boolean">true</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Severity__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">High</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Category__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">Security</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Confidence__c</field>
        <value xsi:type="xsd:string">Medium</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Threshold__c</field>
        <value xsi:nil="true" />
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Description__c</field>
        <value
      xsi:type="xsd:string"
    >A class exposed to users or external systems (@AuraEnabled, @RestResource or webservice) is declared without sharing.</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>Rationale__c</field>
        <value
      xsi:type="xsd:string"
    >Code reachable from the UI or an API then ignores record sharing for the calling user, who may read or change records they should not access. It can be intentional, but must be justified and compensated by explicit checks.</value>
    </values>
    <values>
        <field>SuggestedAction__c</field>
        <value
      xsi:type="xsd:string"
    >Declare the entry point with sharing (or inherited sharing) and move the code that really needs elevated access to a dedicated, narrowly scoped class.</value>
    </values>
</CustomMetadata>
